timestamp=2026-11-04T21:50:54.497Z level=INFO run=6d5b9917 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So meat/frozen zones had major temp breaches starting around 00:00 on 18 July (meat already high at 00:00: 7.23; frozen breached at 02:00). \n# The early morning dispatch at 03:17:17 on 18 July sent out a large batch of frozen and meat products while temps were already unsafe.\n# That batch is very likely the disposal/spoilage event. The other dispatch on 18 July later (09:44:49 meat, 16:17:10 meat, 19:48:06 dry) happened after temps recovered by ~08:00. The frozen temps went back to normal by 08:00 (-17.57). So later dispatches that day would have been under normal conditions. Dry goods unaffected by cold temp.\n# Also no dairy dispatches on 18 July at all. So most plausible write-off is the entire 03:17 dispatch batch of Meat and Frozen products from 18 July.\n\n# The task says: \"Goods that were disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue. Disposal write-offs are rare and, when they occur, leave an unmistakable trace in the data. Only exclude a dispatch from revenue if you are certain it represents a disposal event — when in doubt, count it as a sale.\"\n\n# This temperature spike coinciding with a large dispatch during unsafe storage is an unmistakable trace of likely spoilage/write-off. So exclude all items dispatched at 03:17 on 2023-07-18 in both Frozen and Meat categories? That seems plausible.\n\n# But let us check if all frozen/meat from that batch are written off, or just some. The pattern is that the entire dispatch during breach is lost. That fits.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
