timestamp=2026-11-04T21:52:07.303Z level=INFO run=6d5b9917 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# What about the other dispatches on 18 July later that day?\n# Meat dispatches at 09:44:49 (Pork Belly) and 16:17:10 (Pork Ribs) — these happened after the cold chain recovered (temps back to normal by 08:00). Should they be excluded? Probably not — they were dispatched under normal conditions. The spoilage event was due to the breach; goods dispatched after recovery are fine.\n# Frozen had no dispatches later on 18 July except maybe none? The frozen dispatches after 18 July happened on 19th, 22nd etc., all fine.\n# The Dry dispatch at 19:48:06 on 18 July — not affected by refrigeration issues.\n# So excluding just the 03:17 batch makes sense.\n\n# Are there any other potential spoilage events? The problem says \"full-month outage — no transaction receipts were recorded.\" But warehouse and sensors continued operating. That means we have sensor data. The key is to identify which dispatched goods were disposed of due to spoilage. Only those with unmistakable trace.\n\n# Is there any other temp breach? Only 18 July event. Everything else looks normal. So only the 03:17 batch is the likely write-off.\n\n# Let us also look at the scale. The excluded sum is 10,000 PLN. Is that everything? Let us compute revenue including all dispatches vs excluding just this batch. That gives us 45,391.\n\n# Another possibility: any frozen/meat goods that remained in storage might have spoiled — but the task says \"calculate Expected Revenue (PLN) — total value of goods dispatched from the warehouse that reached customers as sales. Goods that were disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue.\"\n# Also \"Assumption: Every unit dispatched from the warehouse during July 2023 was either sold or written off due to spoilage. No other outcomes (theft, returns, inter-store transfers) occurred.\"\n\n# So spoilage is only relevant for goods that were *dispatched* and then disposed of. That means the write-off is associated with dispatched goods — likely the batch that was in transit or already dispatched during the breach. That fits the 03:17 batch scenario.\n\n# Let us also consider if all products from the 03:17 batch are certainly spoiled. Meat and frozen products sitting in unrefrigerated conditions for hours — definitely spoil. That’s a clear disposal case. Unmistakable.\n\n# Another check: any other dispatches at exactly the same timestamp? Let us count total dispatches at 03:17 on 18 July — 8 line items as shown. Yes, all are meat/frozen. That’s the only batch at that time.\n\n# So expected revenue is total value of all dispatched goods minus those that were disposed due to spoilage = 55391 - 10000 = 45391.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
