timestamp=2026-11-04T21:53:10.178Z level=INFO run=6d5b9917 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Another interpretation: maybe the spoilage affected goods in storage that were *meant* to be sold? But we only see dispatched quantities. The assumption says \"Every unit dispatched from the warehouse during July 2023 was either sold or written off due to spoilage.\" So for dispatched units, sold vs written off. That means revenue comes from dispatched units that were *sold* (not written off). So write-offs apply to dispatched units. That matches the 03:17 batch — dispatched during breach, likely written off.\n\n# Are there any other dispatches where the product category’s temp was violated around dispatch time? For example, the later meat dispatches on 18 July (09:44, 16:17) — temps had recovered by then. So no. Frozen had no dispatches after breach until 19th when temps were fine. No issue.\n\n# Another possibility: could the entire cold storage breach mean all meat and frozen dispatched on 18 July (or even after, until fully safe) are questionable? But \"when in doubt, count it as a sale\" — we need to be certain. The unmistakable trace is probably tied to the timing of the breach vs dispatch. The 03:17 dispatch happened during the height of the breach. Later dispatches after recovery — not certain spoilage, so count as sales.\n\n# That fits perfectly with excluding only the 03:17 batch.\n\n# So expected revenue = 55391 - (sum of 03:17 batch) = 45391.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
